 ### Proof of Correct Ordering by Induction
-A proof by induction that the direction-dependent Morton order aligns with the near-to-far rendering order for all rays with $(+, +, +)$ direction signs, generalizable to other signs by flipping the scene [S1]. Base case ($\ell=1$): For the first Octree level, the direction-dependent Morton order of the eight voxels for $(+, +, +)$ rays is correct because the most significant bit corresponds to $x$, then $y$, then $z$, matching the ray direction [S1]. Induction hypothesis: Assume $S(\ell)$ is true for some positive integer $\ell$, meaning direction-dependent Morton order is correct for all sparse voxel sets in $V_\ell$ [S1]. Induction step: For $V_{\ell+1}$, voxels in $V_\ell$ evolve by subdividing some voxels and removing others. Subdivision appends three zero bits to the least significant bit, preserving order. Subdivided voxels share the same most significant $3\ell$ bits, and their 8 children follow the base case Morton order, preserving relative order. Removing voxels does not affect remaining order [S1].
